<h3>Snowball: reading the input and following the answer contract</h3>
<p>Snowball frequently fails before completing a reliable geometric calculation. In the original run, 38 answers are
format errors and 17 are scorer-detected refusals; in the standardized run those counts are 39 and 12. The traces include
claims that explicitly supplied atoms or coordinates are absent, confusion between chain and amino-acid identifiers,
and missing or empty <code>FINAL</code> fields. The examples below show that these are not all cosmetic mistakes:
some malformed outputs also contain unsupported guesses or incorrect identifiers. Repetitive sequence enumeration
accounts for some context-exhausted traces, but context exhaustion is much less frequent than failure to use the input
and produce a valid answer. A 256K serving limit does not establish reliable use of a long coordinate prompt.</p>

<h3>Mochi: unfinished computation, repetition and coordinate drift</h3>
<p>Mochi gets 44/88 original and 45/88 standardized questions exactly right. Its largest single failure category is
context exhaustion: 22 and 24 questions respectively. These requests received the full space remaining in 256K;
they are not failures caused by an arbitrary short generation cap. Some traces continue lengthy pairwise calculations,
while others loop over copied residue lists or repeatedly attempt to reread the same entry. Both clash and interface
families score 0/6 in each condition; five of the six standardized clash questions exhaust context.</p>
<p>Finished answers also fail: 19 original and 16 standardized responses are parseable but wrong. Examples include
atom positions that drift from the prompt, an invented coincident atom pair and incomplete contact sets. Only three
additional responses per condition are format errors after separating truncations. One standardized fold-class answer
contains the correct category but places <code>FINAL</code> inside a prose line, so the unchanged parser rejects it.</p>

<h3>Qwen 3.6 27B: incomplete geometric searches and heuristic substitution</h3>
<p>Qwen scores 58/88 in each condition, with 27 parseable wrong answers, two format errors and one context exhaustion
per run. Its main bottleneck here is answer correctness. It scores 0/6 on both severe clashes and interface residues in
both conditions. Across the five parseable interface lists in each run, it finds only 11 of 74 required residue memberships
originally and 13 of 75 under standardized settings, with no extra residues. The different denominators reflect the
different malformed question excluded from each calculation. Across all six ligand-contact lists, it finds 16 of 35
required memberships originally and 17 of 35 standardized, again with no extras. These are aggregate membership
counts over parseable lists, not replacements for the benchmark's strict per-question score.</p>
<p>The examples show omitted contacts, a recognised covalent-bond exclusion that is subsequently ignored, and a
qualitative exposure heuristic used instead of the requested surface-area calculation. Simple coordinate lookup and
several local chemistry families are much stronger. The identical overall totals mask changes in which questions
are correct; they do not establish that sampling settings have no effect.</p>

<h3>Trace evidence</h3>
<p>The mechanisms above are interpretations of the linked examples, checked against the displayed prompts and oracle
answers. The counts and contact-set diagnostics are computed over all selected responses. These examples are a targeted
review, not an exhaustive annotation of every reasoning token or a measured frequency of each mechanism. Differences
between single runs do not isolate temperature effects; the conditions change other controls too.
<a href="questions/failure-analysis.json">Download counts, family breakdowns and example provenance</a>.</p>
